   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.8",
    "topic_title": "Representations of Solutions",
    "stem": "Two particulate models represent homogeneous solutions of the same molecular solute, which does not dissociate. Each solute symbol represents the same number of molecules. Model X contains 6 solute symbols and represents 2.0 mL of solution; model Y contains 4 solute symbols and represents 1.0 mL of solution. Solvent particles are omitted. What is the ratio of the solute concentration in X to that in Y?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "3/2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Particle-count-only trap: dividing 6 by 4 ignores the unequal volumes represented by the models. More solute symbols do not necessarily mean a greater concentration. Fix: Divide each model’s solute count by its represented volume before comparing."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "3/4",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Compare solute symbols per represented volume: X has 6/2 = 3 symbols per mL, while Y has 4/1 = 4 symbols per mL. Because each symbol represents the same number of molecules, the concentration ratio is 3/4."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Volume-multiplication trap: (6 × 2)/(4 × 1) multiplies particle counts by volume. Concentration measures particles per volume, so the represented volume belongs in the denominator. Fix: Write solute count/represented volume for each model."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "4/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-ratio trap: this compares Y’s concentration to X’s rather than X’s to Y’s. The prompt requires X’s concentration in the numerator. Fix: Write concentration of X/concentration of Y before substituting values."
     }
    ]
   },
