   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.8",
    "topic_title": "Representations of Solutions",
    "stem": "A student draws particulate diagrams of two aqueous glucose solutions. Diagram 1 shows a container labeled 1.0 L with 8 dissolved glucose molecules. Diagram 2 shows a container labeled 0.50 L with 5 dissolved glucose molecules. Water molecules are omitted and each drawn molecule represents the same number of moles. Which statement correctly compares the molarities of the two solutions?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Solution 1 has the greater molarity, by a factor of 1.6.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This counts drawn particles (8 versus 5) without checking that the diagrams show different volumes. Concentration depends on particles per liter, not total particles drawn. Fix: Divide each particle count by its labeled volume before comparing."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Solution 1 has the greater molarity, by a factor of 3.2.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This multiplies particle count by volume (8 × 1.0 = 8 versus 5 × 0.50 = 2.5), which treats volume as if it increases concentration. Molarity is n/V, so a larger volume dilutes the solute. Fix: Write M = n/V and check that volume sits in the denominator."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Solution 1 has the greater molarity, by a factor of 1.25.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This inverts the ratio, computing liters per particle (1.0/8 = 0.125 versus 0.50/5 = 0.10) and treating the larger value as more concentrated. A larger volume per particle means a more dilute solution. Fix: Check units; concentration must be particles per liter, not liters per particle."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Solution 2 has the greater molarity, by a factor of 1.25.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Molarity is moles of solute per liter of solution, so particle counts must be divided by volume. Diagram 1 gives 8/1.0 = 8 particles per liter, and Diagram 2 gives 5/0.50 = 10 particles per liter. Since 10/8 = 1.25, Solution 2 is more concentrated."
     }
    ]
   },
